            <div xml:id="d226">

                <pb facs="0355" n="256" sameAs="frus:frus1936v05/pg256"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">632.6231/71: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gibson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-05-30T14:00:00-03:00">May 30, 1936—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 7:23 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>138. My 137, May 29, 9 p.m. I succeeded in running Macedo Soares to earth this
                    morning and secured an undertaking that signature would not take place on the
                    subject unless the Department had had an opportunity to examine the agreement
                    with Germany and express its views.</p>
                <p>I am reporting more fully by a later telegram but inasmuch as I have secured this
                    assurance I would suggest that Aranha be advised <pb facs="0356" n="257"
                        xml:id="pg257"/> as there would appear to be no further necessity for him to
                    deal with this phase of the matter.</p>
                <p>Macedo is under heavy pressure from Germany for an early signature and would,
                    therefore, greatly appreciate it if the Department would give immediate
                    consideration to the matter and afford him its views at the earliest moment.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gibson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
